task: merge-chunked-order
agent: opus5-intent
started: 2026-09-09
branch: feature/merge-chunked-order
worktree: ../okay-wt-mergeorder

why: the only known REAL defect open. Found by gate-honesty's own
  gate and filed rather than guessed at; taking it now because a
  finding nobody chases is the worst state a finding can be in — the
  same reasoning that made actor-stop-drain today's most valuable
  work.

the evidence, and why it is not one of the day's false alarms:

    okay.TestStream "merge(chunked): the same elements as merge ..."
    obtained: 1..16, 49, 50, 17..48   (source a's OWN elements)

  - nothing lost, nothing duplicated: 100 elements, both `.sorted`
    assertions passed. So not truncation.
  - 0.068 s. So not starvation, which is what kafka (237 s against
    0.084 s alone) and intent (39 s against 0.059 s) were.
  - nothing early or extra, which is what the two okay.ui failures
    were: a `Thread.sleep` used as a synchronisation point.

  A later chunk of ONE source overtook an earlier chunk of the SAME
  source. Five isolated runs of the suite passed, so it wants
  contention.

THE QUESTION IS THE CONTRACT, and it must be settled before any fix.
  `Source.merge`'s doc is long — chunk size, capacity, `flushAfter`,
  why the size is not a parameter — and says NOTHING about order
  within a source. Two outcomes, both legitimate:

  a) per-source order IS the law. Then the chunked path has a race
     and the code is wrong.
  b) it is not promised for `chunked = true`. Then the TEST asserts
     more than the contract — and the contract must then SAY SO out
     loud, because "merge" naturally reads as interleaving sources
     without reordering within one. Silently weakening the test would
     leave the next reader with the same wrong expectation.

DISQUALIFYING EVIDENCE, registered before the work:

  - no failing repro, no fix. Five isolated runs are green, so a
    green run proves nothing here. Today's method applies: when the
    CONDITIONS cannot be reproduced, reproduce the MECHANISM — for
    the ui pair that meant setting a sleep to zero, and it worked in
    a minute after a 28-spinner load harness had failed.
  - I must not decide the contract by reading my own expectation into
    it. If the design intent is (b), the merge is correct and my
    instinct is the thing that is wrong.
  - `Chunks.merge` is a DIFFERENT function the doc points at for
    already-chunked streams. Whatever I find must be attributed to
    the right one.

predict: (a) — a race, in how chunks reach the channel rather than in
  the merge's own logic, and most likely around the `capacity /
  ChunkSize` slot arithmetic the doc describes. Recorded so being
  wrong shows.

--- FOUND, 2026-09-09 ---

REPRODUCED, which the claim demanded first. 500 merges of the failing
shape: 32 broken, and the first one identical to the gate's
(1..16, 49, 50, 17..48). A/B on the buffer, same probe:

    okay.channel.buffer=ring       0 / 500
    default (growing)             32 / 500

THE PREDICTION WAS WRONG, and in an instructive direction. Not the
`capacity / ChunkSize` arithmetic and not the chunked path. Scope,
measured (ProbeMergeScope, 500 each):

    elementwise, capacity 64 (the DEFAULT)   21
    elementwise, capacity 4                  23
    elementwise, capacity 1<<21               0
    chunked,     capacity 64                 11
    chunked,     capacity 1<<21               0

The plain elementwise merge breaks its sources' order at its own
default capacity. It is not a chunking bug; the chunked test is
merely where it was caught. Above MaxRing the channel takes
`Segments` and orders by one tail, which is why the big capacities
are clean.

THE MECHANISM, reproduced with no merge, no chunks, no streams at all
(ProbeProducerOrder): two producer FIBERS into `Channel[Int](4)`,
each pushing its own arithmetic sequence.

    growing   producer A 12 / 300,  producer B 117 / 300
    ring      producer A  0 / 300,  producer B   0 / 300

    a bad run: ... 12 14 16 18 2 4 6 ...   B's later elements first

One producer alone breaks NOTHING (0 / 300): the buffer only
partitions once two different producers have been refused, and
growing-onep's exemption for resumed pushes holds. It takes two to
grow it, and then:

  `AdaptiveFifo.route()` is `mine.get.idx`, and `mine` is a
  THREAD-LOCAL — "it pushes to the same object for its whole life",
  where the life is a THREAD's. `SentinelChannel.sendAsync` reads it
  once per send and carries it through every park, which is right and
  is the growing-onep fix. But a parked sender is resumed on the
  thread that FREED the slot — the consumer's — and the producer
  fibre's NEXT send then reads the route on that thread and gets a
  different part. Its earlier elements are in the old part; parts
  drain independently; the later element comes out first.

  Correct within a send. Wrong across the sends of one fibre.

THE CONTRACT IS SETTLED: (a). Twice over.

  - `merge` is the READINESS merge — "the loser of every race simply
    arrives later". Later, not out of its own order. `Chunks.merge`
    says "elements in exactly the same order" outright, and every
    library that ships this combinator promises it.
  - and, decisively, `growing`'s OWN doc, written 2026-09-08 when it
    became the default: "WHAT IT COSTS ... EXACT FIFO ACROSS
    PRODUCERS. ... each producer keeps its own order and nothing is
    promised between them." Cross-producer order was given up
    deliberately. Per-producer order was kept, out loud, and that is
    the promise the measurement above breaks.

So there is nothing to weaken in the test. The code is wrong.

--- THE MECHANISM, EXACTLY ---

One line in `Growing.pushDecidingAt`, and its own comment says what
it does:

    // the route was taken from the OLD buffer; ask the new one for
    // this producer's own part rather than reusing a stale index
    grownTo.nn.pushDecidingAt(grownTo.nn.route(), a, unless, orElse)

At the swap every producer is handed a part of its own while its
earlier elements stay in the ring that just became part 0. Parts
drain independently, so those earlier elements can be read after the
later ones. `AdaptiveFifo`'s own comment had the invariant right and
the code broke it: "it pushes to the same object for its whole life".

Fibre migration is NOT needed and was a wrong first guess: plain
platform threads, which never migrate, broke just as hard (A 0 / B
73 in 300). One producer alone breaks nothing (0 in 300) because the
buffer never partitions -- it takes two to grow it.

--- THE FIX ---

READ THE ADOPTED PART FIRST while it holds anything. Everything in
part 0 was pushed before anything in a part opened after it, so
taking part 0 first puts every producer's own elements back in
order, and no producer waits for it.

Two earlier attempts, both rejected by measurement, both recorded in
the code:

  1. Make the PRODUCER wait instead -- keep part 0 until its own
     elements are gone. Rejected by an existing law it broke ("a
     second producer grows it, and what was already in it stays
     readable"): a producer that had pushed NOTHING into the ring
     cannot be told from one that had, so it queued behind a full
     part -- the very stall growth exists to end.
  2. A one-shot barrier that lifts the first time part 0 comes up
     empty. Rejected by the probes at volume: 1 run in 400, one
     element behind its own successors (`... 27 29 31 33 23 35 37`).
     A send whose route was read from the ring BEFORE the swap can
     still arrive at part 0 after the lift. So it is a RULE, not a
     phase, and there is no window left.

Also: the adopted part is now drain-only, its former owner included.
Pinning that producer to part 0 (what the doc described) meant it
REFILLED the part the ordering rule waits on, so on an endless source
it need never be seen empty.

AFTER, every probe, same instruments:

    two plain threads       0 / 2000   (was 73 / 300)
    two virtual threads     0 / 2000   (was 225 / 300)
    two fibres              0 / 2000   (was 117 / 300)
    merge, all five shapes  0 / 500    (was 21, 23, 0, 11, 0)

Two laws added to `TestGrowing`, both proved red with the fix off:

  - "each producer's own order survives the swap" -- the shipped
    `Channel(4)`, two producers, 200 rounds. Probabilistic: it needs
    the buffer to grow and the parts to be read in an unlucky order.
  - "an adopted part is read before the parts opened after it" --
    structural and deterministic. Without the fix the adopted part
    came out LAST: 300..303, 200..203, 100..103, 0..3.

Contract written down where a caller reads it: `Source.merge`
("EACH SOURCE KEEPS ITS OWN ORDER"), `Channel`'s growing-default note
(the per-producer half is a law and is tested as one), and both
corrected paragraphs in `Growing`.

okayJVM/test: 749 passed, 0 failed.

--- WHAT IT COSTS ---

Hard to measure honestly tonight: the box carried five to seven
sibling builds all evening. The first A/B was thrown away for the
right reason -- `adaptive_chunk` is a CONTROL lane the fix cannot
touch (its buffer adopts nothing) and it moved 31% to 75% between the
two halves, which measures the machine.

So the control became the admission test: a round counts only if
`adaptive_chunk` at 16 producers lands within 5% of 318us. Four of
seven rounds did. Minimum per lane, us per run:

                      grow@2   grow@16   control@16
    rule ON  (fix)     153.2     102.9      319.2 / 316.3
    rule OFF           155.3     114.7      319.2 / 317.3

No cost this lane can see. The ON side is also the faster minimum,
and I am NOT claiming a speedup from it: the same lane produced
bars of +/-309 in one of those very rounds, so 10% is inside its
noise. Two valid rounds per side is thin, and it is what the box
allowed.

Structurally the rule costs one `pop` on an empty ring per read, and
only on a buffer that has actually grown -- `adopted` is a plain
final `val` that is false everywhere else, including every
`adaptive`/`relaxed` buffer and every channel with one producer.
